Align the echo controller again when the audio device restarts recording by itself

Patch 0009 left the echo controller as it was when the audio device itself starts again
in a call (a device chosen, a sound server back), as it does when a stream is added or a
track muted: none of those was seen to move the echo. On macOS it does. WebRTC-SDK's
AudioEngineDevice stops and starts its AVAudioEngine, recording with it, whenever
another output or input is chosen, the system's default output or input changes, or the
system changes the engine's configuration, and the new engine plays and records with
another delay between them: AEC3's estimate went from 12-13 blocks to 7-11 after each
such restart (2 to 6 blocks less), and it kept the old alignment until its delay
estimator found the new one, 2 to 5 s later. Meanwhile the other side's voice went back
to them: on a Mac mini with its speakers, the application choosing the monitor's output
and the speakers again in the middle of a call, the other side's speech came back in
bursts of 0.4 to 1.2 s, all within 2 s of the choice and peaking at -30 dBFS (the same
speech reaching the microphone with nothing to cancel it averaged -31 dBFS), in 2 of the
4 returns to the speakers measured; a system's change of default output did the same
once, 1.2 s at -24 dBFS. The patches of 0009 to 0011 restart or remake the echo
controller only as the audio state starts recording, which these restarts do not pass
through.

A device module that starts recording again by itself while recording runs for a call
now marks it (MarkAudioDeviceRestarted, api/audio/audio_device.h: a count for the
library, beside patch 0011's), and the audio processing reads the count at each recorded
block, before its locks, and again under them: when it moved, it aligns the echo
controller again as for a microphone turned on again in the call (patch 0010: what was
played and queued is dropped, the render buffer and delay estimator start over from the
delay the device reports, both filters and the ERLE start over, the suppressor's and the
residual echo's models and the transparent mode stay), also when a change of
devices was marked. A start of recording asked for by the audio state restarts the echo
controller already and takes the count as seen, so it is not followed by another; a
restart marked after it (playout started right after recording, say) gives one more,
one block later. With no echo controller and none wanted (the echo canceller off), the
count is taken as seen and nothing is made. One count for the process: a restart of
another device module (a second factory's) aligns this one's echo controller too, which
costs what an unmute costs.

Measured on Linux in the call lab (tools/call-lab/restart-echo.sh: the application
choosing the other output every 20 s with the other side's speech playing and each
output looped back into the microphone, 12 changes per rig, in two rigs: the loop's
delay moving between the outputs, and the same delay on both): before, the other side's
speech came back in 22 of the 24 changes (11 of 12 in each rig), for 0.25 and 0.45 s at
the median (up to 0.6 s); with this patch in none of the 24 for 0.2 s or more, a remainder
of 50 to 200 ms coming back right at the change. Making the echo controller anew at each
restart instead was worse than doing nothing: the speech came back in 24 of 24 changes,
for 2.2 and 2.3 s at the median (up to 4.7 s), while AEC3 learned the room again. What it costs: in
double talk, a word started within the first second after the change loses about 4 dB
more (8.8 dB at the median against 4.9); with a headset (no echo path) nothing changes.

Measured on Windows (a PC and a virtual machine, the Windows copy choosing another output
in the call between the virtual cable's two outputs, CABLE Input and CABLE In 16ch, both
going back into its microphone: a virtual cable, not a room; 12 changes per build,
interleaved): before, the other side's speech came back at every change, at -17.5 to -27
dBFS for 300 to 500 ms, where the speaker's own words reached the other side at -27 to
-30 dBFS; with this patch nothing in 4 of the 12 and 100 to 200 ms (once 600 ms) at -22
to -38 dBFS in the others; each change marked once and aligned again once.

Marked here: nothing; the macOS engine device marks its restarts (apple patch 0010), the
wrapper its own restarts of the streams on Linux and Windows for a device chosen while
recording runs (wrapper patch 0026), and PulseAudio's module its own (patch 0015).
Windows' Core Audio module starts nothing again by itself while recording runs. Wrapper
patch 0025 tests this patch with the audio state.

diff --git a/api/audio/audio_device.h b/api/audio/audio_device.h
index 0343e67..2136855 100644
--- a/api/audio/audio_device.h
+++ b/api/audio/audio_device.h
@@ -38,6 +38,22 @@ inline void MarkAudioDevicesChanged() {
   AudioDevicesChangedCount().fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
 }
 
+// Fogata: how many times an audio device module started recording again by itself
+// while recording ran for a call: its streams or its engine stopped and started
+// again for another device chosen, a change of the default device, or a device
+// back. The time from what is played to what is recorded may move with it, and
+// the echo controller's alignment with it: the audio processing reads the count
+// at each recorded block and, when it moved, aligns the echo controller again
+// (patch 0014). Not for a start of
+// recording asked for by the audio state (StartRecordingForCall does that).
+inline std::atomic<uint64_t>& AudioDeviceRestartsCount() {
+  static std::atomic<uint64_t> count{0};
+  return count;
+}
+inline void MarkAudioDeviceRestarted() {
+  AudioDeviceRestartsCount().fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
+}
